#!/usr/bin/env python3
from pathlib import Path
Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The CLI helper class (`ScriptInfo` in `src/flask/cli.py`) provides both mechanisms to support different use cases and levels of control in Flask\'s application loading system.\\n\\n**Direct callable factory parameter (`create_app`):**\\n- Used when you have programmatic control over app creation, such as in custom Click scripts or testing scenarios\\n- Allows passing the factory function directly to `ScriptInfo` or `FlaskGroup` (see lines 566, 589, 671)\\n- Provides immediate, explicit control without requiring import path resolution\\n- Example from tests (line 268): `ScriptInfo(create_app=create_app)` where `create_app` is a function defined in the same scope\\n\\n**Import path parameter (`app_import_path`):**\\n- Used when the app location is specified via command-line (`--app` option) or environment variables (`FLASK_APP`)\\n- Supports the discovery-based approach where Flask automatically finds apps or factories in modules\\n- Enables the CLI to work with apps defined in separate modules without requiring direct function references\\n- The `--app` option callback (line 440-446) sets `app_import_path`, which then gets processed by `locate_app()` (lines 344-349) to find apps or factory functions like `create_app` or `make_app` in the target module\\n\\n**Priority and fallback logic:**\\nThe `load_app()` method (lines 333-372) implements a priority system:\\n1. First checks if `create_app` callable is provided (line 341) - highest priority for programmatic control\\n2. Then checks if `app_import_path` is provided (line 344) - for command-line/environment-based discovery\\n3. Finally falls back to searching for `wsgi.py` or `app.py` files (lines 351-356) - for convention-based discovery\\n\\n**Reflection of Flask\'s philosophy:**\\nThis dual-path approach reflects Flask\'s philosophy of providing flexibility and choice:\\n- **Programmatic control** for advanced users who want explicit control (custom scripts, testing frameworks)\\n- **Discovery-based loading** for convenience and convention-over-configuration (standard CLI usage)\\n- **Backward compatibility** with existing patterns while supporting the application factory pattern\\n\\nThe application factory pattern (documented in `docs/patterns/appfactories.rst`) allows creating multiple app instances with different configurations. The dual mechanisms support this by:\\n- Allowing direct factory functions for programmatic instantiation\\n- Supporting import-based discovery that can locate and call factory functions in modules\\n- Enabling both patterns to coexist, giving developers the choice of how to structure and load their applications\\n\\nThis design allows Flask to serve both simple use cases (automatic discovery) and complex scenarios (custom scripts, testing, multiple instances) without forcing a single approach on all users."}' + '\n')
